---
import type { ImageMetadata } from "astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
// Italics for the <em> on this page only: Geist Mono for the lead under the
// heading, Montserrat for the text. The rest of the site does not load them,
// and font-synthesis is off, so emphasis elsewhere stays upright.
import "@fontsource/geist-mono/500-italic.css";
import "@fontsource/montserrat/500-italic.css";

// My bookshelf, in the order it shows on the page: the books in the
// "Book PDFs" folder of my papers-vault, roughly grouped by topic.
// `cover` names a file in src/assets/books/ (without .webp); a book without
// one gets a plain typographic cover instead.
const bookshelf = [
  { title: "Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces", author: "Remzi & Andrea Arpaci-Dusseau", topic: "Operating Systems", cover: "operating-systems-three-easy-pieces" },
  { title: "Understanding the Linux Kernel", author: "Daniel P. Bovet & Marco Cesati", topic: "Operating Systems", cover: "understanding-the-linux-kernel" },
  { title: "Systems Performance", author: "Brendan Gregg", topic: "Performance", cover: "systems-performance" },
  { title: "Site Reliability Engineering", author: "Google", topic: "Infrastructure", cover: "site-reliability-engineering" },
  { title: "Database Internals", author: "Alex Petrov", topic: "Databases", cover: "database-internals" },
  { title: "Designing Data-Intensive Applications", author: "Martin Kleppmann", topic: "Distributed Systems", cover: "designing-data-intensive-applications" },
  { title: "Building Microservices", author: "Sam Newman", topic: "Distributed Systems", cover: "building-microservices" },
  { title: "Building Event-Driven Microservices", author: "Adam Bellemare", topic: "Distributed Systems", cover: "building-event-driven-microservices" },
  { title: "Fundamentals of Data Engineering", author: "Joe Reis & Matt Housley", topic: "Data Engineering", cover: "fundamentals-of-data-engineering" },
  { title: "Designing Machine Learning Systems", author: "Chip Huyen", topic: "Machine Learning", cover: "designing-machine-learning-systems" },
  { title: "Deep Learning with PyTorch", author: "Stevens, Antiga, Huang & Viehmann", topic: "Deep Learning", cover: "deep-learning-with-pytorch" },
  { title: "Mathematics for Machine Learning", author: "Deisenroth, Faisal & Ong", topic: "Mathematics", cover: "mathematics-for-machine-learning" },
  { title: "The Algorithm Design Manual", author: "Steven S. Skiena", topic: "Algorithms", cover: "the-algorithm-design-manual" },
];

const covers = import.meta.glob<{ default: ImageMetadata }>("/src/assets/books/*.webp", { eager: true });
const coverOf = (name?: string) => name ? covers[`/src/assets/books/${name}.webp`]?.default : undefined;
---
<BaseLayout title="About">
  <div class="container article-page">
    <div class="eyebrow">about</div>
    <h1>Hello, Cami</h1>
    <p class="article-lead"><em>Curious, open-minded and slightly hyperactive</em></p>

    
  <div class="prose about-prose">
      <p>
       I’m Camily, a computer scientist based in Curitiba, Brazil. 
       I really enjoy learning how things work under the hood, beyond 
       the abstractions we interact with every day. 
       I want to develop a deeper technical understanding of the 
       things I work with, really getting to know the fundamentals and 
       the mechanisms behind them rather than just knowing how to use them.
      </p>

      <p>
        I have a suspicious number of unfinished side projects and
        a very limited ability to look at an interesting idea and
        think: <em>"maybe I don't need to do anything with this"</em>.
      </p>

      <p>
        I tend to think from first principles. If I can understand
        the basic pieces of something, I feel like I can reason my
        way through the rest. There's a quote I really like:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        If you know the first principles of something, you can
        build the rest of your knowledge around them to produce
        something new.
      </blockquote>

      <h2>Technical interests</h2>

      <p>
        On the technical side, I have a particular weakness for
        distributed systems, backend engineering, DevOps,
        infrastructure, data engineering, machine learning,
        and LLMs.
      </p>

      <p>
        I like turning to reference books to build a stronger technical
        foundation and deepen my understanding of the things I'm learning.
        I share my experiments on <a href="https://github.com/albrescamily" rel="me noopener">GitHub</a>,
        and you can find the books I keep coming back to on my <a href="#bookshelf">bookshelf</a> below.
      </p>

      <h2>Beyond the keyboard</h2>

      <p>
        I graduated in 2025, and I've spent a good part of my
        life moving between different cities. I've lived in eight so far, which I
        think made me pretty adaptable and gave me an early
        sense of how big the world actually is.
      </p>

      <p>
        My interests tend to wander between philosophy,
        technology, art, psychology, current events, and
        completely random ideas I stumble across at 1 a.m.
        Even when I decide not to dive deeper into something,
        I still feel the need to write it down somewhere,
        just in case.
      </p>

      <p>
        Outside of tech, I like video games, movies, music,
        books, and trying out new hobbies. Basically, it's
        just <em>"that looks interesting, I should try it"</em>.
      </p>

      <p>
        I played fingerstyle guitar for a few years. I've
        also tried painting, drawing, and writing short
        stories. More recently, I bought a Swiss Army knife
        because I decided to learn whittling and make small
        wooden sculptures. I have a funny story from my first
        few weeks of learning it... SPOILER: it ended with a
        big scar on the palm of my hand.
      </p>

      <p>
        I also really enjoy working with other people and
        bouncing ideas around, even though I can be a
        little reserved at first. If you ever need someone
        for a hackathon team, I'm probably in.
        I'm always up for talking about tech and ML, too.
        Feel free to reach out on <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/camily-albres" rel="me noopener">LinkedIn</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="bookshelf">My bookshelf</h2>
      <p>
        A collection of technical books I keep coming back to. 
        Some read, some in progress, all worth keeping around.
      </p>
      <ul class="bookshelf">
        {bookshelf.map(book => {
          const cover = coverOf(book.cover);
          return (
            <li class="book">
              {cover
                ? <Image
                    class="book-cover"
                    src={cover}
                    alt={`Cover of ${book.title}`}
                    data-caption={`${book.title} — ${book.author}`}
                    widths={[72, 144, cover.width]}
                    sizes="72px"
                  />
                : <div class="book-cover book-cover-plain" aria-hidden="true">{book.title.split(":")[0]}</div>}
              <div class="book-info">
                <span class="book-title">{book.title}</span>
                <span class="book-author">{book.author}</span>
                <span class="book-topic">{book.topic}</span>
              </div>
            </li>
          );
        })}
      </ul>


    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
